{
    "background_story": "Lea was a brilliant and fearless entomologist, known for her fascination with the world's most reviled creatures. Her colleagues often joked that she had a stomach made of steel, given her ability to handle even the most disgusting specimens. Lea's lab was a treasure trove of creepy-crawlies, from giant hissing cockroaches to flesh-eating beetles. Her magnum opus was a comprehensive study on the mating habits of flies, which earned her both acclaim and notoriety in the scientific community. While many found her subject matter gross, Lea saw beauty in the intricacies of these maligned creatures. She spent years advocating for the importance of entomology in understanding ecosystems and developing new medicines. Lea's passion and dedication eventually won over even the most skeptical of critics, earning her a reputation as a leading expert in her field.",
